    title: "Swedish party blocs tied after Sunday vote, projections say",
    summary: "A partial count by Sweden's election authority projected that left-wing opposition bloc would win a one-seat majority.",
    preview: "Sweden's general election remains too close to call, early results suggest. With 50% of the votes counted, the two main blocs were separated by only 20,000 votes, Sweden public broadcaster SVT said. Projected results from Sweden's election authority had given the left-wing political bloc led by the Social Democrats' Magdalena Andersson a one-seat majority over the rival four-party coalition put together by current conservative Prime Minister Ulf Kristersson.",
    body: "Sweden's general election remains too close to call, early results suggest.\n\nWith 50% of the votes counted, the two main blocs were separated by only 20,000 votes, Sweden public broadcaster SVT said.\n\nProjected results from Sweden's election authority had given the left-wing political bloc led by the Social Democrats' Magdalena Andersson a one-seat majority over the rival four-party coalition put together by current conservative Prime Minister Ulf Kristersson.\n\nKristersson has been supported in government by the hard-right Sweden Democrats (SD), which have neo-Nazi roots. He has promised cabinet jobs for the SD if he stays in charge.\n\n- Close race in Sweden as hard right eyes role in government\n\n- Published1 day ago\n\nBut partial results suggest the party was losing support for the first time since 2010 when it entered parliament.\n\nSweden has two major political blocs featuring four parties on the right and four on the left - meaning coalition and minority governments are relatively common.\n\nOn Sunday night, Andersson urged supporters to wait for the final result, but added that if the current results hold, \"Sweden will have a new government.\"\n\nHer party, the Social Democrats, have been a major force in Swedish politics since the end of World War Two.\n\nBut SD - founded by Nazi sympathisers in the 1980s - had been shunned for years until 2022 when it became the country's second largest party after campaigning on violent crime and immigration.\n\nAfter casting her vote on Sunday, Andersson laid out the choice facing the Swedish public: \"Should we have a government entirely dominated by the Sweden Democrats, something that has never happened in Sweden before? Or should we have a government led by me, which will steer Sweden in a new spirit of co-operation?\"\n\nKristersson, for his part, said it was important he and his alliance were able to continue their work.\n\n\"I think there is a genuine enthusiasm for us being able to continue our important work,\" he said after casting his ballot in his home municipality of Strangnas.\n\nNational security, gang crime and welfare spending dominated the election campaign.",
